<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keladi Nayakas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1499–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Nayakas of Ikkeri, Ikkeri Nayakas, Nayakas of Keladi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a>, also called the Nayakas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a>, were a dynasty that ruled the coast and western hills of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> from about 1499 to 1763, first as governors under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and then independently. Their capitals were successively Keladi, Ikkeri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bidanur</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> conquered their kingdom in 1763.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keladi-nayakas.jpg" alt="Keladi Nayakas" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. SHASHIDHAR of the Indian Administrative Service and N. SREENIVASA MURTHY Geographer of the DCD, Karnataka. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greatest_Extent_Of_Keladi_Kingdom_during_the_reign_of_Shivappa_Nayaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dynasty, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bednur">Bednore</a>, was based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> in the present-day Shimoga district. Its founder, Chaudappa Nayaka, originally Chauda Gowda, came from the village of Pallibailu near Keladi, the son of farmers, and rose to be a feudatory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>. His successor Sadashiva Nayaka earned the title Kotekolahala from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aliya-rama-raya">Aliya Rama Raya</a> for his part in the battle of Kalyani, brought the coastal provinces under his direct rule and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a>, some 20 km from Keladi. The rulers were of the Vokkaliga and Banajiga castes and Veerashaivas by faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the fall of Vijayanagara in 1565 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> became independent rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malnad">Malnad</a> hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>, most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> coast and the plains along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra River</a>. Hiriya Venkatappa Nayaka refused tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penukonda">Penukonda</a> rulers by 1613, defeated the Portuguese in 1618 and 1619 and received the Italian traveller Pietro Della Valle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> in 1623. Under Virabhadra Nayaka the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> army plundered Ikkeri, and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivappa-nayaka">Shivappa Nayaka</a> deposed him in 1645. Shivappa's campaigns against Bijapur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the Portuguese, whose coastal forts he captured, brought the kingdom to its largest extent, and his revenue schemes were later praised by British officials.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi-chennamma">Keladi Chennamma</a>, who ruled from 1672 to 1697, sheltered the Maratha Rajaram when he fled the Mughal army. Wars with local chieftains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and harassment by the Marathas drained the treasury, and Queen Virammaji was defeated and confined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> until the Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhavrao-i">Madhavrao I</a> freed her in 1767.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> built temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikkeri">Ikkeri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keladi">Keladi</a> in granite, mixing late Kadamba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a>, Vijayanagara and Dravida styles, with yali pillars of rearing lions and horses; the Aghoreshwara temple at Ikkeri and the Rameshwara temple at Keladi are the best examples. They spread Veerashaivism through the hills and the coast, with sixty-four mutts in Dakshina Kannada alone, while granting land to other faiths and appointing Nawayath Muslims to office. Basavappa Nayaka wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> encyclopedia Sivatattva Ratnakara, and a member of the family founded the Haleri Kingdom of Coorg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nayakas of Keladi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Keladi Nayakas</b> began as local chiefs at Keladi in the Malnad hills, serving the Vijayanagara Empire as governors from around 1499.</blockquote><a href="/state/keladi-nayaka-kingdom">Keladi Nayaka kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1600 a member of the Haleri family, a Lingayat house related to the <b>Nayakas of Ikkeri</b> (Keladi), established himself in Kodagu, the hilly country at the source of the Kaveri.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-coorg">Kingdom of Coorg</a></li></ul></section>
